\DOC X86_ADD_RETURN_NOSTACK_TAC

\TYPE {X86_ADD_RETURN_NOSTACK_TAC : thm * (thm option array) -> thm -> tactic}

\SYNOPSIS
Given machine-code execution data and a core correctness theorem for a
subroutine body, discharges a top-level {ensures} specification that
additionally reads the stack pointer and return address and ends at the return
address, usually closing the goal outright.

\DESCRIBE
{execth : thm * (thm option array)} is the machine-code execution data for the
program (a length theorem together with the per-instruction decoding array),
used to step the final return instruction; {coreth : thm} is the correctness
theorem for the core (body) of the routine, whose postcondition ends at the
last instruction of the code rather than at the return address. The goal is a
universally quantified implication (possibly under an outer existential) whose
conclusion is an {ensures} statement with precondition mentioning the stack
pointer and the stacked return address and postcondition asserting the return
address and the incremented stack pointer. The tactic strips the quantifiers
and hypotheses, matches the body specification against {coreth}, and executes
the return. In almost all cases no subgoals remain:
{
           A ?- t
   ======================================  X86_ADD_RETURN_NOSTACK_TAC execth coreth
}
\noindent In one observed case it instead leaves a single residual subgoal
about the event trace, with the quantifiers stripped and the assumption list
extended with the instantiated side conditions of the hypotheses, the
loaded-code and memory/register reads for the post-return state, a {MAYCHANGE}
relation between states, and the computed event list, the conclusion being the
remaining existential obligation.

\COMMENTS
The observed traces show only these two outcomes, and the inputs are uniformly
s2n-bignum style {ensures} goals of the shape described above.

\ENDDOC
